  "Terraform Plan": makeSections([
    r("terraform plan proposed infrastructure changes ni apply mundu preview cheyyadaniki use chestaru.", "Terraform plan creates a preview of proposed infrastructure actions without applying those changes to managed infrastructure."),
    r("Terraform configuration, current state, and provider refresh information compare chesi create, update, replace, or destroy actions calculate chestundi.", "Terraform evaluates configuration and state, queries providers as needed, and calculates the actions required to move infrastructure toward the desired configuration."),
    r("Production pipeline lo terraform plan output engineer review chestadu; unexpected database replacement unte apply stop chestaru.", "A production pipeline generates a plan for a network or database change, publishes the proposed actions for review, and blocks deployment when unexpected destructive changes appear."),
    r("Plan input configuration, state snapshot, provider schema, dependency graph, refresh results, and planned actions meeda depend avuthundi.", "A plan depends on configuration, state, provider schemas, dependency relationships, refresh results, and the proposed create, update, replace, or destroy operations."),
    r("Format and validate run chesi plan generate cheyyali, output review cheyyali, approved plan file ni controlled apply stage ki pass cheyyali.", "Format and validate configuration, generate a plan, inspect every proposed action, obtain required approval, and apply the reviewed saved plan when appropriate."),
    r("Commands: terraform plan, terraform plan -out=tfplan, terraform show tfplan, terraform plan -refresh-only.", "Use terraform plan for a preview, -out to save a plan, terraform show to inspect a saved plan, and refresh-only planning when intentionally reconciling state."),
    r("CI pipeline lo plan output artifact ga store chesi approval stage tarvata same saved plan apply cheyyachu.", "A CI workflow can save a plan artifact, expose it for review, and pass the exact saved plan to a controlled deployment stage after approval."),
    r("Plan output chudakunda apply cheyyadam, stale plan use cheyyadam, and destroy or replacement actions ignore cheyyadam dangerous.", "Common mistakes include applying without review, using an outdated plan, overlooking replacement or destroy actions, and assuming a plan guarantees runtime application success."),
    r("Plan fail ayithe syntax, provider auth, refresh errors, dependency graph, and detailed error location inspect cheyyali.", "When planning fails, inspect validation output, provider authentication, refresh errors, dependency references, resource arguments, and the first underlying error."),
    r("Production plan review mandatory cheyyali; destructive changes highlight cheyyali; plan artifact access and retention control cheyyali.", "Require plan review for production, highlight destructive actions, protect plan artifacts, and regenerate plans whenever configuration or relevant state changes."),
    r("Saved plan files sensitive information contain cheyyachu. Secure artifact storage and restricted access use cheyyali.", "Saved plan files can contain sensitive configuration and values. Store them securely, restrict access, and avoid publishing them in public build logs."),
    r("Plan preview matrame; actual resource changes execute cheyyadu. Apply stage changes perform chestundi.", "The plan command previews intended actions; it does not perform them. Apply is the execution step, and provider or runtime errors can still occur during execution."),
    r("Terraform Apply, State, Provider, Dependency Graph, and Infrastructure Change Approval related concepts.", "Related concepts include terraform apply, state refresh, provider behavior, dependency graphs, change approvals, and deployment pipelines."),
  ]),

  "Terraform Apply": makeSections([
    r("terraform apply approved plan actions execute chesi infrastructure ni desired configuration state ki update chestundi.", "Terraform apply executes the proposed infrastructure operations through configured providers to create, update, replace, or destroy managed resources."),
    r("Apply dependency graph order follow avuthu provider API calls execute chestundi and successful changes tarvata state update chestundi.", "Terraform executes resource operations according to dependency relationships, communicates with providers, and updates state as operations complete."),
    r("CI/CD production stage reviewed Terraform plan ni apply chesi network and compute resources deploy chestundi.", "A controlled deployment stage applies a reviewed infrastructure plan to provision approved networking and compute changes in a production account."),
    r("Apply process lo plan, approval, provider authentication, resource operations, state updates, and execution logs untayi.", "The apply process includes plan evaluation or saved-plan input, authorization, provider operations, dependency ordering, state updates, and execution results."),
    r("Validate and plan run cheyyali, destructive changes review cheyyali, approval obtain chesi apply execute chesi infrastructure verify cheyyali.", "Validate the configuration, generate and review the plan, obtain approval, execute apply, inspect output, and verify the actual infrastructure and application health."),
    r("Commands: terraform apply, terraform apply tfplan, terraform apply -auto-approve. Auto-approve production lo strict controls tho matrame use cheyyali.", "Use terraform apply for an interactive apply or terraform apply with a saved plan. Auto-approve skips the interactive approval prompt and should be restricted to controlled automation."),
    r("CI pipeline saved plan artifact ni approval tarvata terraform apply tfplan command tho execute cheyyachu.", "A pipeline can apply a previously reviewed plan artifact using terraform apply with that saved plan, provided the plan remains valid for the current configuration and state."),
    r("Blind auto-approve, wrong workspace, stale plan, insufficient API permissions, and concurrent applies common mistakes.", "Common mistakes include applying in the wrong workspace or account, bypassing review, using stale plans, lacking permissions, and running conflicting deployments."),
    r("Apply failure vaste partial resource changes, provider error, state status, and actual cloud resources inspect chesi reconcile cheyyali.", "An apply can fail after some operations succeed. Inspect the error, state, and real infrastructure before retrying; do not assume failure means nothing changed."),
    r("Production approvals, saved plans, locking, deployment logs, rollback or recovery plans, and controlled execution identities use cheyyali.", "Use approval gates, reviewed plans, backend locking, audit logs, controlled identities, and documented recovery procedures for production applies."),
    r("Apply identity least privilege ga undali. Destructive operations, public resources, and sensitive changes ki additional approval require cheyyali.", "Limit the apply identity to necessary actions and require stronger approval for destructive operations, public exposure, access-policy changes, and sensitive resources."),
    r("Apply changes execute chestundi; plan changes preview chestundi. Successful apply tarvata infrastructure verification still required.", "Plan previews proposed actions, while apply executes them. A successful apply does not replace application-level, security, and availability verification."),
    r("Terraform Plan, Terraform State, Provider, Module, Backend, and CI/CD approval process related concepts.", "Related concepts include plans, state, providers, modules, backends, deployment approvals, and infrastructure drift detection."),
  ]),

  "Ansible": makeSections([
    r("Ansible servers configuration, package installation, service management, and application deployment automate cheyyadaniki use chestaru.", "Ansible is an automation tool used to configure systems, install packages, manage services, and orchestrate application deployment across multiple hosts."),
    r("Control node playbook execute chestundi; inventory target hosts identify chestundi; modules remote tasks perform chestayi, usually SSH dwara.", "Ansible reads playbooks and inventory on the control node, connects to managed hosts, and uses modules to perform the requested tasks."),
    r("DevOps team Ansible playbook use chesi multiple Linux application servers lo same package versions and service configuration maintain chestundi.", "A team uses one playbook to install a required runtime, deploy application configuration, and restart services consistently across a group of Linux servers."),
    r("Control node, managed nodes, inventory, playbook, tasks, modules, roles, variables, and plugins Ansible architecture components.", "Ansible architecture includes a control node, managed nodes, inventory, playbooks, tasks, modules, roles, variables, and plugins."),
    r("Inventory prepare cheyyali, connectivity test cheyyali, playbook define cheyyali, syntax check run chesi controlled hosts meeda execute cheyyali.", "Create inventory, verify connectivity, write a playbook, run syntax checks, test against a limited host group, and then execute across the approved fleet."),
    r("Commands: ansible --version, ansible-inventory --list, ansible all -m ping, ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check.", "Useful commands include ansible --version, ansible-inventory --list, ansible all -m ping, and ansible-playbook with --syntax-check or --check for appropriate dry-run testing."),
    r("Playbook YAML lo hosts, become, vars, and tasks define chestaru. Tasks lo apt, package, copy, template, and service modules use cheyyachu.", "An Ansible playbook defines target hosts and tasks in YAML. Modules such as package, copy, template, and service manage common system operations."),
    r("YAML indentation errors, wrong inventory groups, missing variables, privilege issues, and non-idempotent shell tasks common mistakes.", "Common mistakes include incorrect YAML indentation, wrong host patterns, undefined variables, missing privilege escalation, and shell commands that are not idempotent."),
    r("Playbook fail ayithe verbose output, inventory match, SSH connection, Python availability, module arguments, and privilege escalation inspect cheyyali.", "Troubleshoot failed playbooks by checking verbose task output, matched hosts, SSH connectivity, remote Python, module parameters, and become permissions."),
    r("Idempotent modules prefer cheyyali, roles reuse cheyyali, check mode test cheyyali, and secrets Ansible Vault lo protect cheyyali.", "Prefer idempotent modules, organize reusable roles, test changes with check mode where supported, use handlers, and protect secrets with Ansible Vault or an external secret manager."),
    r("SSH keys, least-privilege sudo, encrypted variables, restricted inventory access, and audited automation credentials use cheyyali.", "Secure Ansible with controlled SSH identities, least-privilege privilege escalation, encrypted secrets, restricted inventory access, and auditable automation credentials."),
    r("Ansible agentless architecture use chestundi for common SSH-managed Linux hosts. Playbooks desired tasks define chestayi.", "Ansible is commonly agentless for SSH-managed Linux hosts. Playbooks describe tasks, while modules perform operations on managed systems."),
    r("Ansible Inventory, Playbook, Role, Module, Variable, Handler, and Configuration Management related concepts.", "Related concepts include inventories, playbooks, roles, modules, variables, handlers, Ansible Vault, and configuration management."),
  ]),

